.page wc 1 count lines, words, characters and bytes

# SYNOPSIS
*wc* [*-lwcmL*] [_FILE_...]

# DESCRIPTION
`wc` counts what is in each _FILE_ (standard input if there is none) and prints one line per file. With more than one file a `total` line follows. Without options it prints lines, words and bytes. The numbers always come in the order lines, words, characters, bytes, longest line.

A word is a run of characters that are not white space.

# OPTIONS
@ *-l*
    The number of lines (newline characters).
@ *-w*
    The number of words.
@ *-c*
    The number of bytes.
@ *-m*
    The number of characters. A UTF-8 character that takes several bytes counts once.
@ *-L*
    The length of the longest line; a tab counts as moving to the next multiple of eight.

When exactly one count is requested for one file only the bare number is printed.

# EXAMPLES
    wc -l /Hakurei/etc/passwd
    ls /bin | wc -l
    echo "naïve" | wc -m -c

# EXIT STATUS
0, or 1 if a file could not be read.

# SEE ALSO
cat(1), head(1), grep(1)
